Phase 2 — Verify active-environment behavior

After Phase 1 is green, create and run the dedicated active-environment tests.

Verify that installation writes exactly one marker:

<active-installed-tree>/config/active_environment

The only permitted values are:

dev
pat
prod

Prove:

* DEV writes and loads dev;
* PAT writes and loads pat;
* Production writes and loads prod;
* all three profiles may be installed, but only the marked profile is active;
* --environment is an assertion and cannot override the marker;
* missing, empty, unknown, padded, multi-line and symlinked markers fail closed;
* profile CLUE_ENV must match the marker;
* RUNTIME_CONFIG must remain beneath the active installed tree;
* runtime YAML environment must match the marker;
* rollback restores the previous marker and matching configuration;
* validation happens before Pillar retrieval or provider activity.

Phase 3 — Close the main-application environment gap

Do not stop after proving the installer and wrapper.

Trace the environment into the real Python entry point:

active_environment
→ deployment profile
→ RUNTIME_CONFIG
→ operational wrapper
→ batch.py
→ envsettings.py
→ selected runtime YAML
→ provider-client construction

The main Python application must receive an explicit environment and absolute runtime-config path from the validated wrapper context.

Before secret retrieval, input processing or provider construction, the application must prove:

active marker
== wrapper environment
== deployment profile CLUE_ENV
== runtime YAML environment

Installed execution must fail closed if this context is missing or inconsistent.

Do not infer the environment from hostname, current user, current directory, endpoint name, profile order or a DEV default.

